WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:10.650
<v Ira Glass>We've been doing a lot of stories lately on the radio show about police. And they've been pretty heavy, we know. This is not a story like that. In fact, kind of the opposite.
<v Ira Glass>最近我们在广播节目中做了很多关于警察的报道，我们知道这些故事都很沉重。但今天这个故事不一样，事实上，甚至可以说是恰恰相反。

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:20.870
<v Ira Glass>When Miles and Katie's apartment was robbed, they knew this was not going to be a big case. And if you got right down to it, they did not have a problem with that. New York City cops arrived after the burglary.
<v Ira Glass>当迈尔斯和凯蒂的公寓被盗时，他们知道这不会是什么大案子，说到底，他们对此也并不介意。案发后，纽约市警察赶到了现场。

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:31.180
<v Miles>They had probably just come from some sort of situation where someone had gotten stabbed in the neck. And now they're in these kind of yuppies' apartment, and we're running down this laundry list of things that had been stolen.
<v Miles>他们可能刚处理完某个人脖子被捅伤的现场，现在却来到了这些雅痞的公寓里，听我们像念清单一样列举被偷的东西。

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:32.120
<v Ira Glass>And the list is like--
<v Ira Glass>那清单简直是——

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:39.060
<v Miles>Oh, it's so embarrassing. It's like, an iPad, an iPod, an Xbox.
<v Miles>哦，太尴尬了。就是iPad、iPod、Xbox这些东西。

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:39.860
<v Ira Glass>They've got bigger problems.
<v Ira Glass>他们有更重要的问题要处理。

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:41.940
<v Katie>Yeah, they got bigger fish to fry.
<v Katie>没错，他们有更棘手的事情要做。

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:57.010
<v Ira Glass>Surveillance video from the building showed that the culprits were three teenagers who took the elevator to the top floor, probably jumped down onto a balcony, and then walked into Katie's and Miles' apartment through a sliding glass door. Then there's video of them leaving the building.
<v Ira Glass>楼里的监控录像显示，作案的是三个十几岁的少年，他们乘电梯到了顶层，可能跳到了阳台上，然后通过滑动玻璃门走进了凯蒂和迈尔斯的公寓。之后还有他们离开大楼的录像。

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:00.430
<v Katie>Three teenage guys with three of our backpacks, stuffed full.
<v Katie>三个十几岁的男孩，背着我们三个装得满满的背包。

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.110
<v Ira Glass>Wow, so they're using your backpacks?
<v Ira Glass>哇，所以他们是用你们的背包搬东西的？

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:03.170
<v Katie>Our own backpacks.
<v Katie>用我们自己的背包。

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:05.750
<v Ira Glass>So they didn't even have any plans on how they're going to carry this out?
<v Ira Glass>所以他们连怎么搬走这些东西的计划都没有吗？

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:06.360
<v Katie>I guess not.
<v Katie>我猜没有。

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:14.520
<v Miles>It didn't seem like a very well-thought-out caper. This was more of a-- let's break into this building and see if someone's dumb enough to leave their sliding glass door open.
<v Miles>这看起来不像是个精心策划的作案。这更像是——让我们潜入这栋大楼，看看有没有人笨到把滑动玻璃门留着没锁。

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:25.230
<v Ira Glass>Of all the things they lost, the only thing that they actually cared about was their video camera. And they only cared about that because it had their wedding video on it-- the only copy. Katie filmed the wedding to show her brother but hadn't yet.
<v Ira Glass>在所有丢失的东西中，他们唯一在意的就是摄像机。而在意的唯一原因就是里面存着他们婚礼的视频——那是唯一的拷贝。凯蒂拍下婚礼是为了给弟弟看，但还没来得及。

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:34.990
<v Katie>My brother, who I'm really close to, couldn't come to the wedding. And so it was really sentimental, and I really wanted it back. And I was really upset. I was really, really sad about it.
<v Katie>我弟弟没能参加婚礼，我和他感情非常好。所以那段视频对我很有纪念意义，我真的很想找回来。我当时真的非常、非常难过。

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:46.030
<v Ira Glass>So the morning after the burglary, knowing the police were not going to be much help on this one, Katie decides to jump into action herself. She goes onto Craigslist to see if the thieves have put her camera up for sale there.
<v Ira Glass>盗窃案发生的第二天早上，凯蒂知道警察在这件事上帮不上什么忙，于是决定自己行动。她上了分类信息网站Craigslist，看看小偷有没有把她的摄像机放在上面卖。

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:57.350
<v Ira Glass>And boom, she finds it immediately. Same camera in the same bag, with all the accessories that she had specially bought for the camera. It had to be hers. And she calls the detectives on the case, tells them the good news.
<v Ira Glass>砰，她立刻就找到了。同样的摄像机，在同样的包里，还有她专门为摄像机买的所有配件。那一定是她的。她打电话给负责案子的侦探，告诉他们这个好消息。

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:27.110
<v Katie>Like, I can get my camera, and I can probably put these guys in jail. And he just immediately shoots me down and says, you know, "there's no way of really knowing if that's your camera. We can't help you with anything. The only way we'd be able to help you is if you got the guy to give you a serial number off the camera and if you happen to still have the serial number from the camera, and they match." And I think he was just thinking, like, that'll never happen.
<v Katie>我想，我可以找回我的摄像机，甚至可能把这些人送进监狱。但他立刻泼了我冷水，说：“你没法确定那是不是你的摄像机。我们帮不了你。除非你能让那个人告诉你摄像机的序列号，而且你刚好还留着摄像机的序列号，并且它们匹配。”我想他当时心里肯定在想，那种事永远不会发生。

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:43.640
<v Ira Glass>Katie, however, saves all her warranties in a special folder. She does have the serial number. And to confirm that it's the same as the advertised camera, she calls the guy on Craigslist and she concocts this story that she is a girl who needs to buy the camera for a school project.
<v Ira Glass>然而，凯蒂把所有的保修卡都存在一个专门的文件夹里。她确实有序列号。为了确认广告上的摄像机就是那个，她打电话给Craigslist上那个人，编了一个故事，说自己是个需要买摄像机做学校项目的女生。

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:55.170
<v Ira Glass>She puts on this supposedly tough-sounding Brooklyn accent that I would describe as kind of the Brooklyn accent you would hear performed by a kid actor on the Disney Channel. I made her do this for me. You do not want to hear it.
<v Ira Glass>她装出一种她自认为很厉害的布鲁克林口音，我形容那种口音就像是迪斯尼频道里的儿童演员表演的那种布鲁克林腔。我让她演示给我听过，你绝对不会想听到的。

00:02:55.170 --> 00:03:13.320
<v Katie>And I told him that my boyfriend was going to kick my ass if I spent our last $300 buying a camera that broke the next day. And so, "Do you have the warranty for the camera?" And he said, "No." He was like, "Uh, I got this camera from my friend, so there was no warranty with it."
<v Katie>我告诉他，如果我把我们剩下的300美元拿去买了一台第二天就坏掉的摄像机，我男朋友会揍死我的。所以我问：“你有这台摄像机的保修单吗？”他说：“没有。”他说：“呃，这台摄像机是我朋友给我的，所以没有保修单。”

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:30.120
<v Katie>I was like, "OK, well, do you know, like, is there a way to find out if it's still under warranty?" And he said, "Well, I think you can find out if the camera's under warranty if you go online." And I was like, "OK, well, what do I need to do?" And he was like, "Well, I think you need to have the serial number."
<v Katie>我说：“哦，好吧，你知道吗，有没有办法查出它是否还在保修期内？”他说：“嗯，我想如果你上网查的话，应该能查到摄像机是否在保修期内。”我说：“好吧，我需要做什么呢？”他说：“嗯，我想你需要有序列号。”

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.450
<v Ira Glass>So you didn't actually just ask him for the serial number. You wanted him to suggest the serial number?
<v Ira Glass>所以你并没有直接向他要序列号，你是想让他主动提出来？

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:39.480
<v Katie>Yeah. Yeah.  Because I thought if I asked him for the serial number, then he would know right away what I was trying to do.
<v Katie>是的，是的。因为我想如果我直接要序列号，他马上就会知道我想干什么。

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:39.650
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:42.330
<v Katie>So I was trying to get him to say it.
<v Katie>所以我当时就是想诱导他说出来。

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:52.700
<v Ira Glass>She asked him, "Can you read me the serial number?" And he says he doesn't have the camera with them. He'll have to call her back. And she figures, oh, that's it. The jig is up. He knows she's lying. He will never call back.
<v Ira Glass>她问他：“你能给我读一下序列号吗？”他说摄像机没在身边，他得回头再给她打过来。她心想，完了，被识破了。他知道她在撒谎，他再也不会打过来了。

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:59.310
<v Ira Glass>As Miles points out, this story that she has made up, it really makes no sense at all. Like, OK, she's got a sociopath boyfriend.
<v Ira Glass>正如迈尔斯所指出的，她编的这个故事根本说不通。好吧，她有一个反社会人格的男朋友。

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:06.770
<v Miles>Who would beat up his girlfriend over the very nerdy error of buying a video camera that wasn't under warranty.
<v Miles>谁会因为买了一台没在保修期内的摄像机这种书呆子才会犯的小错而去打女朋友呢。

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:07.220
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑]

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:18.370
<v Miles>[LAUGHS] It wasn't like he was drunk and he found her cheating on him or anything. He was like, he's violent enough to beat somebody up, but only if they buy a video camera that we don't have a warranty for.
<v Miles>[笑] 这又不是他喝醉了发现她在出轨。他好像是那种暴力到会揍人，但前提仅仅是对方买了台没有保修的摄像机。

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:32.720
<v Miles>(IN HOLLYWOOD BROOKLYN ACCENT)  Because God forbid something should happen, you know, and this camera breaks down. "We can't get our money back! We can't get it repaired! You know what's gonna happen." It's like, what?
<v Miles>(用好莱坞电影里的布鲁克林口音) 因为上帝保佑，千万别出什么事，万一这摄像机坏了怎么办。“我们拿不回钱了！修不好了！你知道会发生什么。”这简直是，什么鬼？

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:40.660
<v Ira Glass>And then the guy calls back. Gives her the serial number. Of course it matches. Katie calls the detective to let him know.
<v Ira Glass>然后那个人回电了，给了她序列号。当然，完全对上了。凯蒂给侦探打了电话通知他。

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:51.310
<v Katie>He says, "Oh, really? Well, what should we do?" [LAUGHS] And I was like, "You tell me what we should do. You're the detective."
<v Katie>他说：“哦，真的吗？好吧，我们该怎么办？” [笑] 我说：“你告诉我该怎么办，你是侦探。”

00:04:51.310 --> 00:05:01.250
<v Ira Glass>And he says, "Of course, we'll do a sting. Call the guy and arrange to buy the camera from him. Set a location. We'll be there, too. We'll swoop in. We'll nab him."
<v Ira Glass>他说：“当然，我们要设个局。打电话给那个人，安排买下摄像机，定个地点。我们也会在那儿，我们会冲进去逮捕他。”

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:07.930
<v Ira Glass>15 minutes later, the police arrive to take Katie and Miles to the sting. Katie runs downstairs to meet the detective on the street.
<v Ira Glass>15分钟后，警察赶到接凯蒂和迈尔斯去执行任务。凯蒂跑下楼在街上见到了侦探。

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:23.960
<v Katie>He tells me that there's been a change of plans. He doesn't need my husband to come anymore. He only needs me. I'm not going to meet the burglar face-to-face anymore, because it's not safe. And so he's got a different plan of how we're going to do this.
<v Katie>他告诉我计划有变。他不再需要我丈夫去了，只需要我。我不会再和窃贼面对面了，因为那不安全。所以他有一个关于如何实施这次抓捕的新计划。

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:26.060
<v Ira Glass>At that point, Miles arrives downstairs.
<v Ira Glass>就在那时，迈尔斯走下了楼。

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:38.510
<v Katie>I turn around to him and I just say, "Change of plans. They don't need us both anymore. They just need me. It's not safe for you to go. I'll call you when it's over."
<v Katie>我转向他说：“计划有变。他们不需要我们两个了，只需要我。你去不安全，等结束了我给你打电话。”

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:39.910
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHTER]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:44.410
<v Katie>[LAUGHTER] And he's, like, stunned.
<v Katie>[笑声] 他当时简直惊呆了。

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:46.590
<v Ira Glass>(LAUGHING)  'Cause that's not alarming at all.
<v Ira Glass>(笑着) 因为这听起来一点儿也不让人担心。

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:49.240
<v Katie>(LAUGHING)  Yeah.
<v Katie>(笑着) 是啊。

00:05:49.240 --> 00:06:02.110
<v Ira Glass>Miles watches her get into the car and drive off. Inside the car, Katie says, the detectives tell her the new plan. She's going to stay in the car and talk to the guy on the cellphone, keep him talking as the cops go and grab them-- though they are very skeptical.
<v Ira Glass>迈尔斯看着她上了车开车离开。在车里，凯蒂说侦探告诉了她新计划。她要留在车里，在手机上和那个人通话，在他和警察靠近抓捕他们时一直跟他聊天——尽管他们对凯蒂非常怀疑。

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:10.210
<v Katie>Basically saying, like, "The chance of this working out is so small." Like, "This is never going to happen. Don't get your panties in a bunch."
<v Katie>基本上就是说：“这事儿成功的概率太小了。这种事儿根本不可能发生。别太紧张了。”

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:21.220
<v Ira Glass>She and the  Craigslist guy agree to meet at a CVS drugstore. Cops go inside. Then on the cellphone, Katie asks him, OK, "What are you wearing?" so she can recognize him. She tells him, "OK, I'm in the make-up aisle."
<v Ira Glass>她和那个Craigslist上的人约好在一家CVS药店见面。警察先进入药店。然后凯蒂在手机里问他：“你穿着什么衣服？”这样她能认出他。她告诉他：“好了，我在化妆品货架这边。”

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:32.790
<v Katie>I'm like, "Oh, I'm by the CoverGirl makeup." He starts walking down the aisle, I guess, and he says, "Oh, I still don't see you." And at that point, I hear, "Hands up! You're under arrest."
<v Katie>我说：“哦，我就在CoverGirl化妆品旁边。”我猜他开始走过过道，他说：“哦，我还是没看见你。”就在那时，我听到：“举起手来！你被捕了。”

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:46.990
<v Katie>And the same detectives who were brushing me off the entire ride out there then are walking over to the car with the video camera in hand, high-fiving each other. And I hear them say, like, "That was some real police work. Great detective work."
<v Katie>然后那些在去路上一路敷衍我的侦探们，手里拿着摄像机向车走来，互相击掌。我听到他们说：“那真是地道的警察工作。出色的侦探工作。”

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:54.740
<v Ira Glass>The police could not be more surprised to be making this arrest. And to be fair, as Miles pointed out, it's crazy that it worked.
<v Ira Glass>警察们对于能完成这次抓捕感到无比惊讶。公平地说，正如迈尔斯所指出的，这事能成简直是疯了。

00:06:54.740 --> 00:07:13.940
<v Miles>There's so many different stages where the criminal should've bailed out, you know? Like the fact that she comes up the story about, I need the warranty. He calls her back. He makes the effort to call her back and said, "Didn't you need a serial number for the warranty?" It's like, what? Like, I can't believe that he's playing along with it.
<v Miles>犯罪分子本该在很多不同阶段就放弃的，不是吗？比如她编出那个需要保修单的故事。他回电了，他特意回电说：“你不需要序列号来保修吗？”这简直是，什么？我不敢相信他竟然顺着她的谎言演戏。

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:16.860
<v Ira Glass>Honestly, like, you don't get that level of service from real stores.
<v Ira Glass>老实说，你在真正的商店里都享受不到这种级别的服务。

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:19.440
<v Miles>[LAUGHS] You don't.
<v Miles>[笑] 确实没有。

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.290
<v Ira Glass>See, but that's the whole thing, is that she's an amateur sleuth, but he's also an amateur criminal.
<v Ira Glass>看，这就是重点，她是个业余侦探，但他也是个业余罪犯。

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:24.550
<v Miles>Right.
<v Miles>没错。

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:26.010
<v Ira Glass>And this is the moment where they meet up.
<v Ira Glass>这就是他们相遇的时刻。

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:40.090
<v Miles>Right. So there'd be no way a detective would ever spend the amount of time that it would take to concoct this ruse to go after this camera. So the only way that it could ever be solved would be if two amateurs met up.
<v Miles>对。所以侦探永远不可能花那么多时间去设计一个这种圈套来追回摄像机。所以这件事唯一能解决的途径就是两个业余选手碰上了。

00:07:40.090 --> 00:08:06.880
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, amateurs-- the fact that they are not professionals, that they do not play by the book, that they have time on their hands to try whatever makes it possible for them to accomplish things the pros simply never will. We have three stories, including amateurs stepping into one of the most powerful jobs in the world and one of the most commonplace. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ广播电台的《美国生活》节目，我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天我们的主题是——业余爱好者。他们不是专业人士，他们不按套路出牌，他们有充裕的时间去尝试任何事，这使得他们能够完成专业人士永远无法完成的事情。我们有三个故事，包括业余爱好者走上世界上最有权力的职位，以及处理最平凡的事情。请继续收听。

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:18.510
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐]

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:35.950
<v Ira Glass>Act One, "Theater of War." Sometimes a big professional outfit that is great at one thing takes on a task at which it has no experience at all, and really no special aptitude for in any way. It is a rank amateur. Jack Hitt has this example.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“战争戏剧”。有时候，一个在某一方面很出色的专业大机构，会去承担一项他们完全没有经验，也没有任何特殊天赋的任务。这简直是一个超级票友。杰克·希特带来了这个例子。

00:08:35.950 --> 00:09:00.550
<v Jack Hitt>Back when the first President Bush was in office, Saddam Hussein invaded Kuwait. And for the first time since Vietnam, really, America returned to the battlefield. Derrick Brown felt called. And so he wound up at Fort Bragg, North Carolina, one of the largest military bases in the world. Back then, about 40,000 soldiers-- most famously, the 82nd Airborne and other Ready Units that can be deployed in 18 hours.
<v Jack Hitt>老布什总统在任时，萨达姆·侯赛因入侵了科威特。自越南战争以来，美国首次真正回到了战场。德里克·布朗感到了一种使命感。于是他来到了北卡罗来纳州的布拉格堡，这是世界上最大的军事基地之一。当时那里有大约4万名士兵，最著名的是第82空降师和其他能在18小时内完成部署的快速反应部队。

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:06.700
<v Jack Hitt>Fort Bragg is America's waiting room for war. When Derrick first arrived, he was trained as a paratrooper.
<v Jack Hitt>布拉格堡是美国的战前候车室。德里克刚到那里时，接受的是伞兵训练。

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:39.820
<v Derrick Brown>You know, you'd wake up at 5:00 in the morning. Then you would deploy out to the field. You'd do a jump from 800 feet. I was an artilleryman, so they'd throw the tanks out of the planes. Then they'd throw us out of the planes. So you'd do these mock war exercises where people would try and attack you and kill you, and you'd be out in the cold, and you'd be digging more foxholes, and just seeing how ground down you could possibly be, so that when the time comes for war, your body and mind have been pushed as far as possible.
<v Derrick Brown>你知道，你早上5点起床，然后部署到野外。你要从800英尺高空跳伞。我是炮兵，所以他们会把坦克从飞机上扔下去，然后把我们也扔下去。你要进行模拟战争演习，有人会试图攻击你并杀了你，你在寒冷中挖掘更多的散兵坑，看看你的身体和精神到底能承受多大压力，这样当战争真正到来时，你的身心就已经被推到了极限。

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:43.460
<v Jack Hitt>Every once in a while, there'd be something else-- a volunteer duty.
<v Jack Hitt>偶尔，还会有其他的事情——志愿任务。

00:09:43.460 --> 00:10:07.060
<v Derrick Brown>I remember the captain of our unit, he said he had this strange detail, which was we had to mandatorily send one soldier from our battery to be a part of this musical. And it would last several weeks. You wouldn't have to do any field duty, but you would have to sing and dance.
<v Derrick Brown>我记得我们连的连长说他有个奇怪的任务，我们要强制从连队派出一名士兵去参加一个音乐剧。要持续好几个星期。你不需要执行任何野外任务，但你得唱歌跳舞。

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:21.240
<v Derrick Brown>And everyone thought he was joking. And I raised my hand. And he said, "Great. Report to the Fort Bragg theater today."
<v Derrick Brown>大家都以为他在开玩笑。但我举手了。他说：“太棒了，今天就向布拉格堡剧院报到。”

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.570
<v Jack Hitt>Were you the only person who volunteered for this?
<v Jack Hitt>你是唯一一个自愿参加的人吗？

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:39.500
<v Derrick Brown>I was the only person who volunteered. And they had me sit in this room, and this director first had everyone audition. And he asked if I knew of any songs that I could sing from any musicals. And I said, "Not at all."
<v Derrick Brown>我是唯一自愿的。他们让我坐在房间里，导演先让大家试镜。他问我是否知道任何能从音乐剧中唱的歌。我说：“完全不知道。”

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:53.280
<v Derrick Brown>I had never sang or danced in my life, except while running and singing cadences and Jody calls in the military. And he said, "Well, just sing me something." And I remember singing "Even Flow," by Pearl Jam.
<v Derrick Brown>我这辈子从来没唱过歌跳过舞，除了在军队里跑步时唱的军号和口令。他说：“好吧，就唱点什么吧。”我记得我唱了珍珠果酱乐队的《Even Flow》。

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:54.630
<v Jack Hitt>Can you give a couple of lines?
<v Jack Hitt>你能唱两句吗？

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:56.960
<v Derrick Brown>It's-- [LAUGHS]
<v Derrick Brown>那是—— [笑]

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:57.740
<v Derrick Brown>(SINGING) Even--
<v Derrick Brown>(唱) Even——

00:10:57.740 --> 00:10:59.120
<v Derrick Brown>[LAUGHS]
<v Derrick Brown>[笑]

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:03.800
<v Derrick Brown>(SINGING)   Even flow, thoughts arrive like--
<v Derrick Brown>(唱) Even flow, thoughts arrive like——

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:07.180
<v Derrick Brown>I think I was just impersonating Eddie Vedder the whole time.
<v Derrick Brown>我想我当时一直在模仿埃迪·维达。

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:08.670
<v Jack Hitt>[LAUGHS] OK.
<v Jack Hitt>[笑] 好吧。

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:19.720
<v Derrick Brown>And somehow they thought it was good enough. And it turns out that no one was turned away.
<v Derrick Brown>不知怎么地，他们觉得还不错。结果证明，没有一个人被拒绝。

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:26.560
<v Jack Hitt>In rehearsals, Derrick quickly got a sense that something different was going on here. This was not just your average musical revue.
<v Jack Hitt>在排练中，德里克很快感觉到这里正在进行一些不同的事情。这不仅仅是普通的音乐剧表演。

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:29.460
<v Jack Hitt>What kind of dancing are you doing?
<v Jack Hitt>你们在跳什么舞？

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:52.670
<v Derrick Brown>It's march dancing. I don't know if you've seen it, but it is the director's idea to combine the honor and discipline of military movements with the bravado and panache of Broadway movements.
<v Derrick Brown>这是队列舞。我不知道你有没有见过，但这是导演的想法，结合了军队动作的荣誉和纪律，以及百老汇动作的浮夸和华丽。

00:11:52.670 --> 00:12:20.140
<v Jack Hitt>But the thing Derrick remembers most, more than 20 years later, was not the song-and-dance numbers. It was the stuff in between. There were short films projected on the screen above the performers. And they were opposite of lighthearted entertainment-- grim testimonials about soldiers who'd died. Not in combat, but drunk driving and in other accidents near the base, all done in a dark bluntness one rarely sees outside driver's ed movies.
<v Jack Hitt>但德里克在20多年后印象最深的，不是歌舞表演，而是中间穿插的内容。屏幕上播放着简短的电影。它们与轻松的娱乐截然相反——那是关于士兵死去的悲惨证言。不是死于战斗，而是死于酒驾或基地附近的事故，所有的处理方式都有一种在驾驶课程电影之外很少见到的阴郁直白。

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:36.890
<v Derrick Brown>We'd go from show tune to a video on the screen of a fellow soldier that they'd interviewed talking about how one of their fellow soldiers, their friend, died. And then-- uh-oh, surprise! We would sing a show tune.
<v Derrick Brown>我们要从表演曲目转到屏幕上关于采访士兵讲述他们朋友是如何死去的视频。然后——哦哦，惊喜！我们还要接着唱表演曲目。

00:12:36.890 --> 00:12:57.260
<v Derrick Brown>And it was this progression of dance, sad video, sing a bit, sad video. And then some guys come rappelling out of the ceiling for the closing number at the end of the hour, for the final chance to cheer everyone up so they leave feeling inspired. It was insane.
<v Derrick Brown>这就是那种舞蹈、悲伤视频、唱歌、悲伤视频的循环。然后演出结束前的一小时，一些家伙从天花板速降下来作为压轴，最后一次试图让大家振作起来，这样他们离开时会感到受鼓舞。这太疯狂了。

00:12:57.260 --> 00:13:09.860
<v Derrick Brown>We would have discussions backstage, which is what the-- what are we doing? Why are we doing this? How did this-- how did this thing even come to be?
<v Derrick Brown>我们在后台会讨论，这到底是——我们在做什么？为什么我们要这么做？这到底是怎么产生的？

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:25.780
<v Jack Hitt>Excellent questions. How did one of the most straitlaced, no-nonsense institutions in the entire country, the US Army, end up producing this piece of Dada musical theater? I wanted answers. So I went straight up the chain of command.
<v Jack Hitt>非常好的问题。这个全国最古板、最讲求实效的机构——美国陆军，到底是怎么制作出这种达达主义音乐剧的？我想要答案。所以我直接去询问了指挥链。

00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:35.990
<v General Carl Stiner>Yes, I'm General Carl Stiner, US Army, retired. And I was the Commander of the 82nd Airborne Division during 1987 and 1988.
<v General Carl Stiner>是的，我是卡尔·斯泰纳将军，美国陆军退役。1987年到1988年期间，我是第82空降师的指挥官。

00:13:35.990 --> 00:13:53.280
<v Jack Hitt>General Stiner was at Fort Bragg. And he confirmed that yes, for a while in the '70s, '80s, and early '90s, there was this annual show which the entire base was required to attend. So there were three or four performances a day for a week or so during the Christmas season, which is when accidental deaths spiked.
<v Jack Hitt>斯泰纳将军当时就在布拉格堡。他证实，是的，在70年代、80年代和90年代初，确实有这样一个年度演出，整个基地的人都被要求参加。所以在圣诞季期间，大概一周时间，每天会有三到四场演出，那段时间正是意外死亡率飙升的时候。

00:13:53.280 --> 00:14:11.700
<v General Carl Stiner>We realized that we were having more casualties from off-duty activities-- mainly motorcycles-- than from thousands of parachute jumps and heavy equipment drops. You know, we just needed to do something about it.
<v General Carl Stiner>我们意识到，我们因非值勤活动——主要是摩托车——造成的伤亡比数千次跳伞和重装备空投造成的伤亡还要多。你知道，我们就是得为此做点什么。

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:43.280
<v Jack Hitt>Stiner told me that some years, for every death of a soldier during training exercises, there were almost 10 killed getting drunk and driving crazy. Wartime deaths, we all know about, and after-war PTSD. But this was a whole other kind of statistic-- pre-combat deaths-- which come when tens of thousands of teenagers anxiously awaiting war get pumped up jumping out of planes and firing heavy artillery. How could some beer and, say, a motorcycle possibly affect them?
<v Jack Hitt>斯泰纳告诉我，有些年份，每有一名士兵死于训练演习，几乎就有10个人死于酗酒和疯狂驾驶。战争死亡我们都知道，还有战后创伤后应激障碍。但这完全是另一种统计数据——战斗前死亡——这些发生在上万名焦急等待战争的年轻人身上，他们通过跳伞和发射重型火炮来释放压力。几瓶啤酒，或者说，一辆摩托车怎么可能影响到他们？

00:14:43.280 --> 00:15:03.110
<v Jack Hitt>Originally, the base had ordered mandatory safety lectures. But the soldiers just slept through them. So the United States Defense Department, the greatest fighting force ever to march on the planet, decided they would try show tunes. And that's the origin of what came to be called The Soldier Safety Show.
<v Jack Hitt>最初，基地强制执行安全讲座，但士兵们在讲座上只会睡觉。所以美国国防部，这支地球上行军的最伟大战斗力量，决定尝试音乐剧。这就是后来被称为“士兵安全秀”的起源。

00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:17.690
<v Jack Hitt>I interviewed a dozen veterans from this era, and they all remember the weird mash-up of gut-wrenching testimonials and cheery eye-high leg kicks. They all said, "You had to see it to believe it." And I wanted to believe it.
<v Jack Hitt>我采访了那个时代的十几名退伍军人，他们都记得那种揪心的证言和欢快的踢腿动作之间怪异的融合。他们都说：“你得亲眼见过才敢相信。”而我真的想见识一下。

00:15:17.690 --> 00:15:21.250
<v Jack Hitt>When I called Stiner, he told me that he had a videotape of one of the shows.
<v Jack Hitt>当我联系斯泰纳时，他告诉我他有一盘演出录像带。

00:15:21.250 --> 00:15:35.950
<v General Carl Stiner>You know, I had a VCR of it. But age caused the thing to self-destruct. You couldn't even tell what it was, the pictures and so on. And so we struck out.
<v General Carl Stiner>你知道，我曾经有一盘录像带。但年代久远让它自我毁坏了。你甚至看不出那是什么，画面等等都糊了。所以我们没戏了。

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:54.060
<v Jack Hitt>I won't bore you with the details. But finding a videotape literally took months. The public school library had a set of Soldier Safety Shows, but not long before I called, they went full-on digital and junked all their old school audiovisual stuff. A Freedom of Information request came up completely dry.
<v Jack Hitt>我不会详细描述过程。但找到一盘录像带确实花费了数月时间。公立学校图书馆曾经有一套士兵安全秀，但在我联系前不久，他们全面数字化，把所有的旧视听资料都报废了。信息自由法案的请求也完全无果。

00:15:54.060 --> 00:16:09.340
<v Jack Hitt>Finally, I happened upon Gudron Blackmon, still working at Bragg, who called back to say she had an old box at home and, in it, a tape. The label read, "1989 Soldier Safety Show-- Life is Worth Living."
<v Jack Hitt>最后，我偶然联系到了古德隆·布莱克蒙，她还在布拉格堡工作，她回电说家里有一个旧箱子，里面有一盘带子。标签上写着：“1989士兵安全秀——生命值得活下去。”

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:14.790
<v Folk Guitarist>(SINGING)  This road of life we travel, it can take us many ways.
<v Folk Guitarist>(唱) 我们行走的生命之路，可以带我们去很多地方。

00:16:14.790 --> 00:16:19.590
<v Jack Hitt>Early on, we see a folk guitarist seated onstage, plucking out a sad melody.
<v Jack Hitt>在早期，我们看到一位民谣吉他手坐在舞台上，弹奏着悲伤的旋律。

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:25.150
<v Folk Guitarist>(SINGING)  But for all of these young soldiers whose lives had just begun--
<v Folk Guitarist>(唱) 但是对于所有这些生命刚刚开始的年轻士兵来说——

00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:35.010
<v Jack Hitt>Above him, on a screen, is a general. It's actually General Stiner himself entering a cemetery. And he starts talking about accidental deaths on base.
<v Jack Hitt>在他上方，屏幕上是一位将军。那实际上是斯泰纳将军本人正在进入公墓。他开始谈论基地里的意外死亡。

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:53.730
<v General Carl Stiner>When a soldier is killed, I feel terrible. I get a sickening feeling, a feeling of failure. We lost 26 this year, and it's terrible. Charles. Adam. James.
<v General Carl Stiner>当一名士兵死去时，我感到很糟糕。我有一种令人作呕的感觉，一种失败感。今年我们失去了26人，这很可怕。查尔斯。亚当。詹姆斯。

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:57.690
<v Jack Hitt>General Stiner occasionally kneels down, and, at one point, removes his beret--
<v Jack Hitt>斯泰纳将军偶尔会跪下，并在某一点摘下贝雷帽——

00:16:57.690 --> 00:16:58.590
<v General Carl Stiner>Unnecessary.
<v General Carl Stiner>没必要。

00:16:58.590 --> 00:17:00.610
<v Jack Hitt>And talks directly to a tombstone.
<v Jack Hitt>直接对着墓碑讲话。

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:28.800
<v General Carl Stiner>We tried with you, Tosher, and we failed. Perhaps we could have done a little better if your friends had been more forthcoming to us with your problems. You were a great soldier. And we loved you. How many new graves next year? What do I have to do to stop these unnecessary and needless deaths?
<v General Carl Stiner>我们尝试过帮助你，托舍尔，但我们失败了。也许如果你的朋友们能多向我们反映你的问题，我们会做得更好一点。你是个伟大的士兵，我们爱你。明年还会有多少新坟墓？我该怎么做才能停止这些不必要和无谓的死亡？

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:33.750
<v General Carl Stiner>[JAZZ BAND MUSIC PLAYING]
<v General Carl Stiner>[爵士乐队音乐]

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:55.520
<v Jack Hitt>The lights come up on a stage packed with 30 or 40 men and women in fancy dress doing some tap dance moves with lots of spirit fingers and jazz hands. It's a tribute to everyone who fought in World War II. And these absurd smash-ups just keep happening for the rest of the show, just like all the soldiers told me.
<v Jack Hitt>舞台灯光亮起，上面挤满了30或40名穿着华丽服装的男女，他们跳着踢踏舞，配合着夸张的手势和爵士舞姿。这是对所有二战老兵的致敬。这种荒谬的冲突在演出剩下的时间里一直持续，就像所有士兵告诉我的那样。

00:17:55.520 --> 00:18:07.340
<v Jack Hitt>So who came up with this idea? Who was the mastermind of Operation Schlock and Awe? His name was Lee Yopp. And his journey to theater was not one anyone might predict.
<v Jack Hitt>那么是谁想出这个主意的？“震惊与敬畏行动”的策划者是谁？他的名字叫李·约普。他走上戏剧之路的历程没有人能预见到。

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:21.830
<v Bo Thorp>He had-- he had no personal taste. He ate overdone meat, and he wore polyester suits. I mean, he didn't care what he wore.
<v Bo Thorp>他——他没有个人品味。他吃过熟的肉，穿着聚酯西装。我是说，他不在乎自己穿什么。

00:18:21.830 --> 00:18:34.570
<v Jack Hitt>That's Bo Thorp, a local North Carolina actress who was frequently cast in these shows. Yopp died back in 2006. I talked to other actors, too, and they all reacted like Reese Brown, a paratrooper from the '90s.
<v Jack Hitt>那是博·索普，一位经常被选入这些演出的北卡罗来纳州女演员。约普在2006年去世了。我也和其他演员谈过，他们都像90年代的伞兵里斯·布朗一样反应。

00:18:34.570 --> 00:18:36.510
<v Reese Brown>[CHUCKLES]
<v Reese Brown>[轻笑]

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:41.010
<v Jack Hitt>[LAUGHS] See, everyone laughs when I ask about Lee.
<v Jack Hitt>[笑] 看，我问起李的时候大家都笑了。

00:18:41.010 --> 00:19:03.790
<v Reese Brown>No, because it's funny, because I haven't thought about him in years. But just to think about just some of the things that he said, every word he used, every enunciation he used, every colorful description he used, was filled with 10 words that didn't need to be there. [LAUGHS] He didn't say, "I'm going to the store." "I'm traversing down to--" You know, it was one of those things.
<v Reese Brown>不，因为这很有趣，因为我已经很多年没想过他了。但想到他说的每一句话，他用的每一个措辞，每一个夸张的描述，里面都充斥着10个本不需要在那里的词。[笑] 他不会说“我去商店”。他说“我将穿行前往……”你知道，就是这类事情。

00:19:03.790 --> 00:19:12.300
<v Reese Brown>But he had no room at all for shyness on stage. He made it a point of saying that if you're going to fail, please fail loudly.
<v Reese Brown>但他舞台上根本容不得羞怯。他强调如果注定要失败，请大声地失败。

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:13.230
<v Reese Brown>[LAUGHS]
<v Reese Brown>[笑]

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:15.100
<v Jack Hitt>[LAUGHS]
<v Jack Hitt>[笑]

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:29.470
<v Reese Brown>"Please fail loudly, and sing it as though your life depended on it." [CHUCKLES] It's odd, because I'd never met anyone like him, prior to this point. It's like, where did this guy come from?
<v Reese Brown>“请大声地失败，像生命依赖于它一样去歌唱。” [轻笑] 这很奇怪，因为在此之前我从没见过像他这样的人。就像，这家伙是从哪儿冒出来的？

00:19:29.470 --> 00:19:32.160
<v Bo Thorp>He was a high school football coach.
<v Bo Thorp>他是个高中橄榄球教练。

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:33.690
<v Jack Hitt>That's Bo Thorp again.
<v Jack Hitt>还是博·索普。

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:55.450
<v Bo Thorp>This was in Trenton, New Jersey. And the director of the class play died suddenly, and Lee inherited the class play. And it also happened that he had just broken his leg, so he really couldn't go out on the football field. So that's how he got into the theater.
<v Bo Thorp>那是新泽西州的特伦顿。班级戏剧的导演突然去世，李继承了班级戏剧。恰巧那时他刚摔断了腿，所以他真的不能去橄榄球场。这就是他进入戏剧界的经过。

00:19:55.450 --> 00:20:03.310
<v Steve Currie>And so he took his A personality football coach approach to life into the theater.
<v Steve Currie>于是他把橄榄球教练的那种A型人格带到了戏剧中。

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:06.860
<v Jack Hitt>This is Steve Currie, who long worked as Yopp's stage manager.
<v Jack Hitt>这是史蒂夫·库里，曾长期担任约普的舞台监督。

00:20:06.860 --> 00:20:32.260
<v Steve Currie>And within a very short period of time, turned this little high school theater program into a major competitive organization that won the National Theater Award for high schools across the nation. And they went on a tour of Germany and Japan with this production he put together. And it just was like he was bit, you know? He couldn't get enough of it.
<v Steve Currie>在很短的时间内，他把这个小高中的戏剧项目变成了一个重大的竞争组织，赢得了全美高中的全国戏剧奖。他们还带着他制作的这部剧去德国和日本巡演。就好像他上瘾了一样，你知道吗？他沉迷其中无法自拔。

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:45.880
<v Jack Hitt>Lee Yopp wound up as the chief director of the Bucks County Playhouse outside Trenton, New Jersey, which is one of the farm teams for lots of Broadway shows. Yopp gave Bucks County his all, spending lavishly, even putting his own money into the shows.
<v Jack Hitt>李·约普最终成为了新泽西州特伦顿郊外巴克斯县剧场的首席导演，那是许多百老汇演出的训练基地之一。约普为巴克斯县倾尽所有，大肆挥霍，甚至把自己的钱投进演出中。

00:20:45.880 --> 00:21:09.980
<v Steve Currie>Lee had just taken a second mortgage on his house in New Jersey to use as an investment in a show called Smile, Smile, Smile . The reviews came out. It was Smile , Smile , Smile , it was supposed to be funny. It wasn't, it wasn't, it wasn't. Period. It was one of the shortest theater reviews in the history of musical theater.
<v Steve Currie>李刚刚给他在新泽西州的房子办了二抵，投资了一部叫《微笑，微笑，微笑》的剧。评论出来了。它应该很有趣。结果它不，它不，它不。句号。这是音乐剧历史上最短的剧评之一。

00:21:09.980 --> 00:21:27.330
<v Jack Hitt>Currie told me that Yopp bankrupted the Bucks County Playhouse-- and himself, his personal finances so wrecked, he couldn't get a phone in his own name. But then fate stepped in when Yopp bumped into an old Army buddy at an airport who was looking for someone who could direct theater at Fort Bragg.
<v Jack Hitt>库里告诉我，约普搞破产了巴克斯县剧场——还有他自己，他的个人财务状况彻底崩溃，连自己的名字都不能用来开电话账户。但后来命运降临，约普在机场偶遇了一位老战友，那人正在寻找能为布拉格堡执导戏剧的人。

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:38.590
<v Bo Thorp>They were thrilled to death with this man who brought theatricality to the Army, the last place in the world that you would think.
<v Bo Thorp>他们对这个把戏剧性带入陆军的人感到无比兴奋，这是你认为世界上最后一个会出现戏剧的地方。

00:21:38.590 --> 00:21:41.070
<v Jack Hitt>The one financial backer--
<v Jack Hitt>那个唯一的投资人——

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:45.990
<v Bo Thorp>That-- right, that could afford him. Right. That could afford him. Exactly.
<v Bo Thorp>那个——对了，就是那个付得起他酬劳的人。对了。就是那个付得起他的人。没错。

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:47.380
<v Jack Hitt>The Defense Department.
<v Jack Hitt>美国国防部。

00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:49.200
<v Bo Thorp>That's exactly right.
<v Bo Thorp>完全正确。

00:21:49.200 --> 00:22:02.050
<v Jack Hitt>Steve Currie, the stage manager, told me that with that money, they built waterfalls and a set for a roller-skating number. Another time, they planted the entire stage in real grass for a park scene with cops on horseback.
<v Jack Hitt>舞台监督史蒂夫·库里告诉我，有了那笔钱，他们搭建了瀑布，还有旱冰表演的布景。还有一次，为了一个有骑马警察出现的公园场景，他们在整个舞台上种了真草。

00:22:02.050 --> 00:22:15.240
<v Steve Currie>Oh, we had people rappelling from rafters. We had people in parachute rigging flying in over the stage. Cannons and machine guns and muskets, and horses rearing up through the cannon fire. Thompson submachine guns firing.
<v Steve Currie>哦，有人从房梁上速降。有人穿着降落伞装备从舞台上空飞过。大炮、机枪、火枪，还有马匹在炮火中嘶鸣。汤姆森冲锋枪开火。

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:18.220
<v Jack Hitt>And these are all going off inside a theater.
<v Jack Hitt>这些都是在剧场里发生的。

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:19.760
<v Steve Currie>Oh, yeah. [LAUGHS]
<v Steve Currie>哦，是的。[笑]

00:22:19.760 --> 00:22:21.160
<v Jack Hitt>[LAUGHS]
<v Jack Hitt>[笑]

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:42.500
<v Steve Currie>We would also trigger explosions that we'd set into the stage floor. I would hook up these, like, flash pots. I got-- I got the guy from Special Forces-- he would get me all these, what they call squibs, which are these electronic fuses, and we would create these great explosions. You could never do it today. Fire marshal would shut you down. But we were the Army.
<v Steve Currie>我们还会触发我们在舞台地板里设置的爆炸。我会连接这些类似闪光罐的东西。我让特种部队的人——他会给我弄来所有这些所谓的导爆索，也就是电子引信，我们制造了这些巨大的爆炸。你现在绝对做不到这一点了，消防队长会关掉你的剧场。但我们是陆军。

00:22:42.500 --> 00:22:42.960
<v Jack Hitt>Wow.
<v Jack Hitt>哇。

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:55.090
<v Steve Currie>And what was impressive about it is how I realized that there was no end to Yopp. He had a special and unique way of making the simple more complicated than you could ever imagine it.
<v Steve Currie>令人印象深刻的是我意识到约普没有尽头。他有一种特殊而独特的方法，能让简单的事情变得复杂得超出你的想象。

00:22:55.090 --> 00:23:21.580
<v Jack Hitt>Let me just take a moment to say how rare this is. I wrote a book about amateurs a few years ago, those dreamers in their garages, fiddling, creating, struggling. What keeps most of them stuck in their garage is lack of funders. Lee Yopp had hundreds of millions of funders-- the taxpayers of the United States.
<v Jack Hitt>让我花点时间谈谈这有多罕见。几年前我写了一本关于业余爱好者的书，那些车库里的梦想家，修修补补，创造，奋斗。大多数人被困在车库的原因是缺乏资金支持者。李·约普拥有数以亿计的资助者——美国纳税人。

00:23:21.580 --> 00:23:39.540
<v Jack Hitt>Everybody I talked to-- especially the cast and crew that were in so many of these shows, the regulars-- all mentioned this one time, one moment that stood out for them. It was 1982. There'd been a terrible car accident, this time involving one of the cast members' kids. Here's actress Bo Thorp.
<v Jack Hitt>我交谈过的每个人——尤其是那些经常参与演出的演员和工作人员——都提到了这一个让他们印象深刻的时刻。那是1982年。发生了一起严重的车祸，这次涉及到其中一名演员的孩子。这是女演员博·索普。

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:48.910
<v Bo Thorp>My son was nearly killed. I mean, he was coming home from his first football game. He was 14 years old. It was October the 8th.
<v Bo Thorp>我儿子差点死了。我的意思是，他从第一场橄榄球比赛回家。他当时14岁。那是10月8日。

00:23:48.910 --> 00:24:13.750
<v Bo Thorp>And the young girl who was driving her father's car decided to see how fast her daddy's car would come down Sky Drive, which is where we live. He remembers her saying 80. And when she went to put on the brakes, the car went into a skid and then hit a tree. And so he was in a body cast for six months.
<v Bo Thorp>开着她父亲车的那个年轻女孩决定看看她爸爸的车在天际路——我们住的地方——能开多快。他记得她说时速是80英里。当她想踩刹车时，车子打滑撞上了一棵树。所以他在全身石膏里躺了六个月。

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:20.840
<v Jack Hitt>She says everyone in the show was heartbroken, of course, including Yopp. But Yopp, being Yopp, saw an opportunity.
<v Jack Hitt>她说剧组里的每个人当然都心碎了，包括约普。但约普，终究是约普，他看到了一个机会。

00:24:20.840 --> 00:24:35.080
<v Bo Thorp>He said, "Ah! You know what we can do? You think-- would you let us go up to the hospital? And I'll bring a film crew, and we'll go up to the hospital, and we'll film Clay. And then he can-- and then he can make testimony on the tape."
<v Bo Thorp>他说：“啊！你知道我们可以做什么吗？你认为——你愿意让我们去医院吗？我会带一个摄制组去医院，我们去拍克莱。然后他就能——然后他就能在磁带上作证。”

00:24:35.080 --> 00:24:38.860
<v Bo Thorp>And I said, "Lee, I don't-- I don't know that that's the right thing to do."
<v Bo Thorp>我说：“李，我不知道——我不知道那是该做的事。”

00:24:38.860 --> 00:24:45.040
<v Bo Thorp>"Oh, yeah, absolutely! They'll-- the soldiers will love it. They'll-- they'll understand it.  It'll mean a lot to them."
<v Bo Thorp>“哦，是的，绝对！士兵们会喜欢的。他们会——他们会理解的。这对他们意义重大。”

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:51.170
<v Bo Thorp>You know, I-- I was really reluctant about it, 'cause I thought that, you know, it was just not very tasteful.
<v Bo Thorp>你知道，我——我真的很勉强，因为我觉得，你知道，这不太有品位。

00:24:51.170 --> 00:24:54.970
<v Bo Thorp>[CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]
<v Bo Thorp>[欢呼和掌声]

00:24:54.970 --> 00:24:59.410
<v Jack Hitt>A few weeks later, there was Bo on stage.
<v Jack Hitt>几周后，博出现在舞台上。

00:24:59.410 --> 00:25:20.690
<v Bo Thorp>My 14-year-old son was in a very serious car accident. It happens like that, you know? There's no warning. It just comes-- boom-- like that. We went up to Clay's hospital room when he was very sick. And he agreed to make this film. I hope you'll pay attention.
<v Bo Thorp>我14岁的儿子发生了一起非常严重的交通事故。事情就这样发生了，你知道吗？没有任何预警。就这么——砰——就那样。在他病得很重时，我们去了克莱的病房。他同意制作这部电影。我希望大家关注。

00:25:20.690 --> 00:25:39.150
<v Jack Hitt>Then, on the big screen, Clay Thorp, 14 years old, lying in a hospital bed, flat on his back, in a full body cast. And Clay jumps right in, looking into the camera as best he can, reliving the accident, calling out the miles per hour as if he were once again flying down that hill.
<v Jack Hitt>然后，在大屏幕上，14岁的克莱·索普躺在病床上，平躺着，打着全身石膏。克莱直接看向镜头，尽他所能，重温那场事故，惊叫着时速，仿佛他再次在那山坡上疾驰。

00:25:39.150 --> 00:25:57.610
<v Clay Thorp>10. 20. 30. 40. 50. I told her to slow down. 60? 70? Oh my god! This is a dream. This can't be happening.
<v Clay Thorp>10，20，30，40，50。我叫她慢下来。60？70？天哪！这是场梦。这不可能发生。

00:25:57.610 --> 00:26:11.090
<v Clay Thorp>Here I am in this place. I'm not normal now. I wish I could get out. I wish I could get out tomorrow.
<v Clay Thorp>我竟然在这个地方。我现在不正常了。我希望能出去。我希望明天就能出去。

00:26:11.090 --> 00:26:20.570
<v Clay Thorp>(SINGING) The sun'll come out, tomorrow. Bet your bottom dollar that tomorrow, there'll be sun.
<v Clay Thorp>(唱) 太阳明天会出来。你敢把最后一块钱压在明天，明天会有太阳。

00:26:20.570 --> 00:26:24.560
<v Jack Hitt>And then beneath the video screen, a young girl comes out on stage.
<v Jack Hitt>然后视频屏幕下方，一个年轻女孩走上舞台。

00:26:24.560 --> 00:26:37.530
<v Girl>(SINGING)  When I'm stuck with a day that's gray and lonely, I just stick out my chin and grin and say, oh--
<v Girl>(唱) 当我被困在灰暗孤独的一天，我就伸出下巴咧嘴笑说，哦——

00:26:37.530 --> 00:26:44.020
<v Jack Hitt>And-- and do you think this had-- I mean, how-- how effective was this with the soldiers?
<v Jack Hitt>而且——你认为这有——我是说，这——这对士兵们有多有效？

00:26:44.020 --> 00:26:49.010
<v Bo Thorp>Amazing. Absolutely amazing. Because these are 18-year-old boys.
<v Bo Thorp>惊人。绝对惊人。因为这些是18岁的男孩。

00:26:49.010 --> 00:27:18.950
<v Bo Thorp>And when you're 18 years old and you're a butch kind of male, your life is gonna go on forever. And you're-- you're special. You're gonna last forever. And you can do whatever you want to do. And-- and if you die, so what? And Lee had a way of understanding that. He would do everything he could to prove to them or to show them the value of life and how short it is, and how quickly it goes by.
<v Bo Thorp>当你18岁，是个强壮的男人时，你的生活会永远持续下去。你很特别，你会永远存在。你可以做任何你想做的事。而且——如果你死了，那又怎样？李有办法理解这一点。他会尽一切努力向他们证明或者向他们展示生命的价值，它是多么短暂，消逝得多么快。

00:27:18.950 --> 00:27:25.440
<v Chorus>(SINGING)  The sun'll come out tomorrow. Bet your bottom dollar that tomorrow, there'll be--
<v Chorus>(唱) 太阳明天会出来。你敢把最后一块钱压在明天，明天会有——

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:42.900
<v Jack Hitt>You know what this is? It's the lecture I gave my teenage daughter a few nights ago when I handed her the car keys. "Drive carefully. Buckle up. Please don't be an idiot." Every parent tries to come up with some way to say this to their kid. Lee Yopp had to crack that code for 40,000 of them.
<v Jack Hitt>你知道这是什么吗？这是几天前我把车钥匙交给十几岁的女儿时，我给她的演讲。“小心驾驶。系好安全带。请别犯傻。”每个父母都试图想出某种方式告诉孩子这些。李·约普不得不为4万个孩子破解了这个密码。

00:27:42.900 --> 00:27:51.880
<v Jack Hitt>And you know what? It worked. After the Soldier Safety Show started, the average number of deaths at the base dropped by a third.
<v Jack Hitt>而且你知道吗？它成功了。在士兵安全秀开始后，基地里的死亡人数平均下降了三分之一。

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:57.870
<v Chorus>(SINGING) Tomorrow, tomorrow, I love ya, tomorrow. You're only--
<v Chorus>(唱) 明天，明天，我爱你，明天。你只是——

00:27:57.870 --> 00:28:03.860
<v Jack Hitt>At the end of every show, one of Yopp's actors always came out on the stage to give the final safety lecture.
<v Jack Hitt>在每场演出结束时，约普的演员之一总是会来到舞台上进行最后的安全演讲。

00:28:03.860 --> 00:28:14.840
<v Man>We need your help. And we ask that each and every one of you join with us to make this pledge to always, always wear your safety belts.
<v Man>我们需要你的帮助。我们要求在座的每一位与我们一起做出承诺，永远、永远系好你们的安全带。

00:28:14.840 --> 00:28:20.830
<v Jack Hitt>The speaker asks everyone who believes in safety to stand and take the pledge. These are soldiers, and they follow orders.
<v Jack Hitt>演讲者要求所有相信安全的人站起来做出承诺。他们是士兵，他们服从命令。

00:28:20.830 --> 00:28:21.320
<v Man>Please--
<v Man>请——

00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:36.290
<v Jack Hitt>So naturally, up jumps everyone in the audience. And then the cast floods the stage for the final number and Yopp's last brilliant contribution to musical theater-- a guaranteed standing ovation at the end of every show.
<v Jack Hitt>所以观众中所有人都自然地跳了起来。然后演员们涌上舞台进行最后的演出，这是约普对音乐剧最后也是最辉煌的贡献——每场演出结束时保证起立鼓掌。

00:28:36.290 --> 00:28:51.760
<v Chorus>(SINGING)  Everyone will sing!
<v Chorus>(唱) 每个人都要唱！

00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:53.760
<v Chorus>[CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]
<v Chorus>[欢呼和掌声]

00:28:53.760 --> 00:29:02.740
<v Ira Glass>Jack Hitt. He's the author of several books, including the one that he mentioned in this story. It is called Bunch of Amateurs . It recently came out in paperback.
<v Ira Glass>杰克·希特。他是几本书的作者，包括他在这个故事中提到的那本。书名叫《业余爱好者》。最近出了平装本。

00:29:02.740 --> 00:29:14.220
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, babies having babies-- robot babies, specifically. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，婴儿生婴儿——特别是机器人婴儿。芝加哥公共广播电台将在节目继续时为你播出。

00:29:14.220 --> 00:29:27.690
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme and bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show-- "Amateur Hour," stories of how amateurs can accomplish things that the pros cannot. That is, when they are not crashing and burning completely.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，我们都会选择一个主题，为你带来不同种类的故事。今天的节目——“业余时间”，关于业余爱好者如何能够完成专业人士无法做到的事情的故事。当然，前提是他们没有彻底搞砸。

00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:33.680
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, "What to Expect When You're Expecting a Robot."
<v Ira Glass>现在是节目的第二幕。第二幕，“当你期待一个机器人宝宝时会发生什么”。

00:29:33.680 --> 00:29:58.130
<v Ira Glass>There's this thing that a lot of kids have to do in health class. Maybe you did this when you were in high school. The idea is to give students a taste of what it is like to be responsible for an infant-- and kind of what a pain in the ass it is, actually. And they do this by making the kids carry around an egg-- or sometimes it's, like, a sack of flour-- for a couple days. Supposedly this jolt of reality helps prevent teen pregnancies. Which, you know, is a lot to ask of an egg.
<v Ira Glass>很多孩子在健康课上必须做这件事。也许你在高中时也做过。目的是让学生体会作为婴儿的责任，以及这实际上是多么麻烦。他们通过让孩子带着一个鸡蛋——或者有时是一袋面粉——走来走去几天来实现。据说这种现实的冲击有助于防止青少年怀孕。这，你知道，对一个鸡蛋来说要求太高了。

00:29:58.130 --> 00:30:23.080
<v Ira Glass>So these days, some schools are using these robotic babies. These are plastic dolls  with electronics inside which make them cry randomly, all day and night. Their sleeping, eating, and pooping patterns are based on logs kept by real parents about real babies. The babies cost over $600 each. Hillary Frank went out to find out if amateur parenthood is more intense and realistic with these high-tech crybabies.
<v Ira Glass>所以现在，一些学校开始使用这些机器人婴儿。这些是带有电子装置的塑料玩偶，整天整夜随机啼哭。它们的睡眠、饮食和排便模式都是基于真实父母记录的真实婴儿数据。每个婴儿的价格超过600美元。希拉里·弗兰克去调查这种业余父母身份是否通过这些高科技哭闹娃娃变得更加强烈和逼真。

00:30:23.080 --> 00:30:47.530
<v Hillary Frank>The first time I saw a robotic baby was at my local pharmacy. It was one of those old-timey places with a candy counter and surgical supplies and a large selection of scented candles. One day I was in the store, and I heard this teenage girl behind me say, "My god, this baby is so heavy." She had a car seat slung over her shoulder like a purse. For a second I thought the plastic baby inside it was real.
<v Hillary Frank>我第一次见到机器人婴儿是在当地的药店。那是一家古色古香的商店，有糖果柜台、医疗用品和各式香薰蜡烛。有一天我在店里，听到身后有个十几岁的女孩说：“天哪，这婴儿太重了。”她把汽车安全座椅像钱包一样斜背在肩上。那一秒我以为里面的塑料婴儿是真的。

00:30:47.530 --> 00:31:04.500
<v Hillary Frank>"The real ones are heavier," I told her. She groaned and whipped the car seat over to her other shoulder. At the time I had a three-year-old, and I was dying to know if this girl's experience with the plastic baby was anything remotely like my experience with my flesh-and-blood baby.
<v Hillary Frank>“真的婴儿更重，”我告诉她。她呻吟着，把安全座椅甩到另一侧肩膀上。当时我有一个三岁的孩子，我很想知道这个女孩对塑料婴儿的经历是否与我对自己血肉之躯宝宝的经历有任何相似之处。

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:13.980
<v Hillary Frank>The girl told me she went to Glen Ridge High. Glen Ridge is the town next to mine in New Jersey. So I called the school and asked if they'd let me hang out the next time the girls got the babies.
<v Hillary Frank>那个女孩告诉我她去的是格伦里奇高中。格伦里奇就在我居住的新泽西州小镇隔壁。所以我打电话给学校，询问下次女孩们领取婴儿时是否允许我跟着看看。

00:31:13.980 --> 00:31:17.470
<v Robot Baby>[WAILING]
<v Robot Baby>[啼哭]

00:31:17.470 --> 00:31:22.460
<v Ms. Hogan>Oh, and don't forget to support her neck when you pick her up out of the car seat. OK?
<v Ms. Hogan>哦，别忘了在把她从安全座椅里抱出来时支撑她的脖子。好吗？

00:31:22.460 --> 00:31:37.930
<v Hillary Frank>Ms. Hogan, the health teacher, is setting up two girls with their babies. The robot babies are so expensive that the school only has 10 of them, so the students take turns with them for two solid days of pretend parenting. In the class I visited, the students on deck were Rachel--
<v Hillary Frank>健康老师霍根女士正在为两个女孩准备她们的宝宝。机器人婴儿太贵了，所以学校只有10个，学生们轮流照顾，进行为期两天的模拟育儿。在我访问的班级里，当值的学生是瑞秋——

00:31:37.930 --> 00:31:41.420
<v Rachel>Let's kick it. I'm excited. My baby's here.
<v Rachel>开始吧。我很兴奋，我的宝宝在这儿。

00:31:41.420 --> 00:31:42.420
<v Hillary Frank>And Paige.
<v Hillary Frank>还有佩奇。

00:31:42.420 --> 00:31:46.910
<v Paige>I don't know why I'm so nervous about this baby, but it-- it'll be good.
