<v Robot Baby>(主体) [数码哔哔声]

00:39:53.940 --> 00:39:58.930
<v Rachel>This is the first time she's woken me up. It's 11:30 PM.
<v Rachel>这是她第一次把我吵醒。现在是晚上11:30。

00:39:58.930 --> 00:40:02.920
<v Robot Baby>[CRYING]
<v Robot Baby>[啼哭]

00:40:02.920 --> 00:40:10.400
<v Rachel>Diaper changing and the bottle have not worked. I'm attempting to rock the baby.
<v Rachel>换尿布和奶瓶都没管用。我正尝试摇晃她。

00:40:10.400 --> 00:40:14.890
<v Robot Baby>[CRYING, BUT CALMER]
<v Robot Baby>[啼哭，但冷静下来]

00:40:14.890 --> 00:40:17.390
<v Robot Baby>[STOPS CRYING]
<v Robot Baby>[停止哭泣]

00:40:17.390 --> 00:40:25.870
<v Paige>She appears to have stopped. Hopefully she'll be sated for a while. Rachel, over and out.
<v Paige>她好像停止了。希望她能安静一会儿。瑞秋，完毕。

00:40:25.870 --> 00:40:27.870
<v Paige>(SUBJECT) [SILENCE]
<v Paige>(主体) [静默]

00:40:27.870 --> 00:40:32.860
<v Robot Baby>[SCREAMING AND CRYING]
<v Robot Baby>[尖叫和哭泣]

00:40:32.860 --> 00:40:33.860
<v Robot Baby>(SUBJECT) [DIGITAL BEEP]
<v Robot Baby>(主体) [数码哔哔声]

00:40:33.860 --> 00:40:38.350
<v Rachel>This is wake-up number two, about five minutes later.
<v Rachel>这是第二次惊醒，大概五分钟后。

00:40:38.350 --> 00:40:40.840
<v Robot Baby>[MURMURS]
<v Robot Baby>[嘟囔]

00:40:40.840 --> 00:40:45.830
<v Rachel>It seems like she doesn't really need anything. She's just lonely, I guess.
<v Rachel>看来她什么都不需要。我想她只是寂寞了。

00:40:45.830 --> 00:40:47.330
<v Robot Baby>[COOING]
<v Robot Baby>[咕咕声]

00:40:47.330 --> 00:41:00.300
<v Rachel>(SINGING)  Oh, once I had a little dog. Its color, it was brown. I taught him for to whistle--
<v Rachel>(唱) 哦，我曾有只小狗。它的毛色是棕色。我教它吹哨子——

00:41:00.300 --> 00:41:05.790
<v Rachel>It is 4:13. The baby is hungry again.
<v Rachel>现在是4:13。宝宝又饿了。

00:41:05.790 --> 00:41:07.290
<v Robot Baby>[GULPING]
<v Robot Baby>[吞咽声]

00:41:07.290 --> 00:41:18.770
<v Rachel>[HEAVY SIGH] The tiredness is really setting in. I've grown strangely emotionally attached to this lump of plastic. It'll be a tiny bit sad to get rid of her.
<v Rachel>[重重的叹息] 疲惫感真的袭来了。我莫名其妙地对这块塑料产生了感情。要丢掉她会有点难过。

00:41:18.770 --> 00:41:19.760
<v Robot Baby>[GULPING]
<v Robot Baby>[吞咽声]

00:41:19.760 --> 00:41:31.240
<v Hillary Frank>Rachel records herself every single time the baby wakes her up both nights, a total of nine times. Paige was so overwhelmed by the night wakings that she didn't wind up recording herself at all.
<v Hillary Frank>瑞秋在两晚里每次宝宝吵醒她都记录了下来，总共九次。佩奇被半夜惊醒搞得焦头烂额，甚至没记录下自己。

00:41:31.240 --> 00:41:48.210
<v Paige>Like, I don't-- I am so, so tired. And my whole-- I don't know why, but I feel like my whole body has just been slammed up against a wall. I just-- I can barely keep my eyes open, and I just feel so exhausted.
<v Paige>就像，我不知道——我太累了。我的全身——我不知道为什么，我觉得我的身体好像被重重摔在墙上。我只是——我几乎睁不开眼，我感到非常精疲力竭。

00:41:48.210 --> 00:41:54.200
<v Hillary Frank>And, uh-- and how were you feeling while it was going on? Were you feeling mad at the baby?
<v Hillary Frank>而且，嗯——当它发生时，你感觉如何？你对宝宝感到愤怒吗？

00:41:54.200 --> 00:42:14.160
<v Paige>A little bit. I-- I was like, man, I just-- my Mama came in and said, "Well, scan it, sweetheart." And I remember saying, "I already did." And then I, like, took her diaper off, threw the diaper, put the diaper back on, and she still was crying. And I looked at my mom and said, "Mo-om."
<v Paige>有一点。我——我当时想，天哪，我只是——我妈妈走过来说：“宝贝，扫描一下。”我记得我说：“我已经扫描过了。”然后我取下尿布，扔掉尿布，换上新尿布，她还在哭。我看着妈妈说：“妈——”

00:42:14.160 --> 00:42:24.140
<v Paige>And so-- and around third time she woke up, I was like-- [SNARLS]
<v Paige>大约第三次她醒来时，我想—— [嘶吼]

00:42:24.140 --> 00:42:27.130
<v Rachel>I just wanted everyone to be quiet and leave me alone.
<v Rachel>我只是希望所有人安静，让我一个人待着。

00:42:27.130 --> 00:42:30.620
<v Hillary Frank>The next day at school, Rachel was not herself.
<v Hillary Frank>第二天在学校，瑞秋不像她自己了。

00:42:30.620 --> 00:42:48.590
<v Rachel>Before Chorus, Katie Schultz-- who was actually, she plays the nun in the play. She just asked me, "How's the baby?" And I just went-- (SHRILLY) "Can everyone just shut up?" And in my mind, I was like, she wasn't doing anything. She was not the person I should be yelling at. But I was just like-- [SCREAMS]
<v Rachel>合唱课前，凯蒂·舒尔茨——她在剧中演修女。她只是问我：“宝宝怎么样？”我立刻就—— (尖锐地) “大家能不能闭嘴？”我心里想，她又没做什么。她不是我该吼的那个人。但我只是—— [尖叫]

00:42:48.590 --> 00:43:04.050
<v Hillary Frank>Overnight, Paige and Rachel have become animals I recognize intimately. They've become mothers, throwing their minds, bodies, and hearts so fully into a tiny being that they can't help but whine and lash out at people who don't deserve it.
<v Hillary Frank>一夜之间，佩奇和瑞秋变成了我熟悉的那种动物。她们变成了母亲，将自己的身心和灵魂完全投入到一个微小的生命中，以至于她们忍不住抱怨，并对那些无辜的人发泄。

00:43:04.050 --> 00:43:24.020
<v Hillary Frank>Even Paige says she's become a jerk-- or her version of a jerk. She forgot to thank a boy who held the door for her. Plus, she admits to me that she's cheated a second time. She forgot to bring her costume for dress rehearsal, so she slipped the bracelet off again and left the baby with a friend while she ran home.
<v Hillary Frank>连佩奇都说她变成了一个混蛋——或者说她版本的混蛋。她忘了感谢一个帮她开门的男孩。而且，她向我承认她第二次作弊了。她忘了带演出服，所以她再次脱掉手镯，在跑回家时把宝宝留给了朋友。

00:43:24.020 --> 00:43:34.490
<v Hillary Frank>Next morning comes the moment of truth. The girls hand in their babies and get their grades. The robot baby keeps a minute-by-minute record of how you did. Paige goes first.
<v Hillary Frank>第二天早上是审判时刻。女孩们交还了宝宝，并拿到了分数。机器人婴儿保留了你照顾它的每一分钟记录。佩奇先来。

00:43:34.490 --> 00:43:35.990
<v Ms. Hogan>This is a 71.
<v Ms. Hogan>这是71分。

00:43:35.990 --> 00:43:37.990
<v Hillary Frank>71-- which is what, in letters?
<v Hillary Frank>71分——那换算成字母是什么？

00:43:37.990 --> 00:43:38.990
<v Ms. Hogan>A C-minus.
<v Ms. Hogan>C-minus。

00:43:38.990 --> 00:43:41.480
<v Hillary Frank>And what did she get points deducted for?
<v Hillary Frank>她是因为什么被扣分的？

00:43:41.480 --> 00:43:52.700
<v Ms. Hogan>For-- she had a missed feeding, a head support, and then 6:49, she had a missed feeding and a head support. 6:58, she had a missed burp. And 9:29-- yeah, so.
<v Ms. Hogan>因为——她错过了一次喂食，一次头部支撑，然后在6:49，她又错过了一次喂食和头部支撑。6:58，她错过了一次拍嗝。9:29——是的，所以。

00:43:52.700 --> 00:43:53.050
<v Paige>Yeah.
<v Paige>是的。

00:43:53.050 --> 00:43:53.600
<v Hillary Frank>All right.
<v Hillary Frank>好吧。

00:43:53.600 --> 00:43:55.490
<v Ms. Hogan>It's all right.  Don't worry. Don't fret.
<v Ms. Hogan>没关系。别担心，别烦恼。

00:43:55.490 --> 00:44:14.410
<v Paige>I didn't want all that to happen. 'Cause I-- every time she cried, I picked her up immediately, gave her her bottle. If she didn't want that, I put her diaper on. So I don't know. I thought I did OK. I didn't think I'd get, like, a C. So, a little disappointed.
<v Paige>我不希望这些发生。因为我——每次她哭，我马上就抱起她，给她奶瓶。如果她不想要，我就换尿布。所以我不知道。我想我表现得还可以。我没想到会拿C。所以，有点失望。

00:44:14.410 --> 00:44:18.220
<v Ms. Hogan>Rach, let me get your grade for you.
<v Ms. Hogan>瑞秋，我给你看你的成绩。

00:44:18.220 --> 00:44:20.940
<v Hillary Frank>The big moment for Rachel. She's getting her grade.
<v Hillary Frank>瑞秋的重大时刻。她正在拿成绩。

00:44:20.940 --> 00:44:24.430
<v Rachel>Aaaaah.
<v Rachel>啊啊啊。

00:44:24.430 --> 00:44:25.160
<v Ms. Hogan>94.
<v Ms. Hogan>94分。

00:44:25.160 --> 00:44:26.340
<v Hillary Frank>Which translates to an A?
<v Hillary Frank>这意味着是A？

00:44:26.340 --> 00:44:27.890
<v Ms. Hogan>An A, yeah.
<v Ms. Hogan>是一个A，没错。

00:44:27.890 --> 00:44:45.700
<v Hillary Frank>If I were a betting person, I'd have never put money on Rachel doing better than Paige-- especially not this much better. For all of her baby clothes and maternal instinct-- not to mention wanting to be a young mom-- Paige turns out to be a below-average mother, at least when it comes to plastic children.
<v Hillary Frank>如果我是个会打赌的人，我绝不会押瑞秋比佩奇表现好——尤其好这么多。尽管她准备了婴儿衣服和母性本能——更不用说想要早点成为妈妈——结果佩奇在照顾塑料婴儿方面竟是个低于平均水平的母亲。

00:44:45.700 --> 00:44:51.880
<v Hillary Frank>Has this changed the way you feel about becoming a mother and about babies?
<v Hillary Frank>这是否改变了你对成为母亲和对婴儿的看法？

00:44:51.880 --> 00:45:32.610
<v Paige>Um, I thought, like, I could have a baby at 21. I'll go to ministry school, meet a man of God, a man of my dreams. We'll get married and have a baby. But I think-- maybe-- maybe I think I-- maybe a little older than I thought? Like, I-- I cannot even imagine putting a baby, a life, into my world. And I think it'd be horrible.
<v Paige>嗯，我以前想，比如我在21岁时生个孩子。我会去牧师学校，遇到上帝挑选的人，我梦想中的男人。我们会结婚并生个孩子。但我想——也许——也许我认为我——也许应该比我想的再大一点？比如，我——我甚至无法想象把一个婴儿，一个生命，带入我的世界。我觉得那会很糟糕。

00:45:32.610 --> 00:45:42.450
<v Hillary Frank>Paige isn't sure how old she wants to be when she starts a family now. She just knows she can't even picture it. Her whole life plan has been thrown out of whack by her robot baby.
<v Hillary Frank>佩奇现在不确定她想在多大时组建家庭了。她只知道她甚至无法想象。她的人生规划被机器人宝宝搞乱了。

00:45:42.450 --> 00:45:51.510
<v Rachel>My response to the baby was almost the opposite of Paige's, in that if I have a baby really young, I feel like I would keep the baby.
<v Rachel>我对宝宝的反应几乎和佩奇相反，如果我真的很年轻生孩子，我觉得我会留下孩子。

00:45:51.510 --> 00:45:55.820
<v Hillary Frank>I also never would have guessed that Paige and Rachel would flip their positions.
<v Hillary Frank>我也没料到佩奇和瑞秋会反转位置。

00:45:55.820 --> 00:46:11.070
<v Rachel>Because I kind of like the feeling of having a baby. Like for some reason, like, I guess the idea that someone or something needs you and only you kind of makes you feel all, like, important.
<v Rachel>因为我有点喜欢照顾宝宝的感觉。因为某些原因，比如，我想这种感觉就像是有人或什么东西需要你，而且只需要你，会让你感觉——很重要。

00:46:11.070 --> 00:46:20.690
<v Rachel>It was awful, but at the same time, like, I was-- I kind of liked it. I'd never really thought that I would want to have a kid younger, but maybe I would.
<v Rachel>虽然过程很糟糕，但同时，我——我还挺喜欢的。我从未想过想早点要孩子，但也许我愿意。

00:46:20.690 --> 00:46:38.680
<v Hillary Frank>Rachel called it early-onset baby fever and said her mom was freaked out by it. I asked one of Rachel's teachers, and the president of the company that makes the babies, about this ironic and unintended outcome-- that the robot baby made a teenage girl want to have a real one.
<v Hillary Frank>瑞秋称之为“早发婴儿热”，说她妈妈被吓坏了。我问了瑞秋的一位老师，以及制造这些婴儿的公司总裁，关于这个具有讽刺意味的意外结果——机器人宝宝让一个少女想要生个真实的孩子。

00:46:38.680 --> 00:46:51.820
<v Hillary Frank>They both said this was rare. They shrugged it off. The teacher said she was sure Rachel's the kind of girl who will make a thoughtful decision about when to have her own child.
<v Hillary Frank>他们都说这种情况很少见。他们没放在心上。老师说她确定瑞秋是那种会对自己何时生孩子做出深思熟虑决定的女孩。

00:46:51.820 --> 00:47:10.950
<v Hillary Frank>Overall, the girls had a way more authentic mothering experience with the robot babies than I'd expected. But the moments when Rachel and Paige couldn't be available to the babies-- when Rachel gave blood, when Paige fell-- the girls lost points in those moments. When you're a real parent, those things happen all the time.
<v Hillary Frank>总的来说，女孩们通过机器人宝宝获得了比我预想中更真实的育儿体验。但当瑞秋和佩奇无法对宝宝负责的时刻——当瑞秋献血时，当佩奇摔倒时——女孩们在那些时刻丢了分。当你是一个真正的父母，那些事情一直都在发生。

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:33.730
<v Hillary Frank>Diagnosing whether your kid needs to be fed or diapered or rocked, that's not what makes you a pro at parenting. It's coping with the stress of getting it wrong, of feeling like an amateur. Which is going to happen, no matter how much practice you've had.
<v Hillary Frank>诊断你的孩子是需要喂食、换尿布还是摇晃，这并不能让你成为育儿专家。应对做错事情、感觉自己是个业余选手的压力才是。无论你有多少练习，这种情况都会发生。

00:47:33.730 --> 00:47:54.730
<v Ira Glass>Hillary Frank. She's the host of the parenting podcast The Longest Shortest Time, which I have to say is great, because unlike most parenting stuff, it is not actually designed specifically for parent listeners. It's for anybody. It's distributed by Earwolf.
<v Ira Glass>希拉里·弗兰克。她是育儿播客《最长最短的时间》的主持人，我得说这个播客很棒，因为与大多数育儿内容不同，它不是专门为父母设计的，它是为任何人准备的。由Earwolf分发。

00:47:54.730 --> 00:48:17.910
<v Ira Glass>Act Three. "Commander In Brief." Now we have the story of an amateur who, for one night, gets thrown into a very, very big job, one of the biggest jobs. They basically get thrown in as the substitute teacher for a class they have never taught or never taken-- specifically, they're supposed to sub for the President of the United States. Stephanie Foo explains.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“简报中的指挥官”。现在我们要讲一个业余选手的故事，他在一晚的时间里，被扔进了一个非常、非常重大的职位，可以说是最大的职位之一。他们基本上被扔进去当了代课老师，教的是他们从未教过也从未上过的课——具体来说，他们得代行美国总统的职责。斯蒂芬妮·傅为你解释。

00:48:17.910 --> 00:48:45.340
<v Stephanie Foo>If somebody needs to sub for the President, the chain is clear. If the Vice President can't do it, it goes to the Speaker of the House, then the President pro tempore of the Senate, then the Secretary of State, Secretary of the Treasury, Secretary of Defense, all the way down the Cabinet to Energy, Education, Veteran Affairs. It's been this way since the Presidential Succession Act of 1947. But what happens if all of those people are in one room?
<v Stephanie Foo>如果有人需要代行总统职权，继任链很清晰。如果副总统不能代行，继任者是众议院议长，然后是参议院临时议长，接着是国务卿、财政部长、国防部长，一直排到能源、教育、退伍军人事务部。自1947年《总统继任法》以来一直如此。但如果所有人都在同一个房间里会发生什么？

00:48:45.340 --> 00:48:53.140
<v President Barack Obama>Mr. Speaker, Mr. Vice President, members of Congress, my fellow Americans.
<v President Barack Obama>议长先生，副总统先生，国会议员们，我的美国同胞们。

00:48:53.140 --> 00:49:05.000
<v Stephanie Foo>The State of the Union address. What if someone blows up the building and kills everyone on that list all at once? Well, then the presidency goes to the designated survivor.
<v Stephanie Foo>国情咨文演说。如果有人炸掉大楼，同时杀死了名单上的所有人怎么办？那么总统职位就由“指定幸存者”来担任。

00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:26.100
<v Stephanie Foo>That is a person chosen every year before the State of the Union speech to survive. That's their whole job-- to survive, run the United States government as an amateur president, total beginner, whose first day on the job may include wreaking vengeance upon-slash-surrendering to whoever killed all our top leaders.
<v Stephanie Foo>那是每年在国情咨文演讲前选出的一位幸存者。这就是他们的全部工作——生存下去，作为业余总统管理美国政府，一个完全的新手，其上任的第一天可能包括向杀害我们所有领导人的人寻求报复或投降。

00:49:26.100 --> 00:49:48.680
<v Stephanie Foo>Each year, the designated survivor is assigned a security detail and moved to a safe, secret location away from the Capitol. This all began-- no surprise-- during the Cold War. Thomas Reed was an assistant to President Reagan for national security policy. And it was his job to figure out what to do if a nuclear weapon hit DC. He's the one who came up with the idea of the designated survivor.
<v Stephanie Foo>每年，指定幸存者会被分配一个安保分队，并被转移到远离国会大厦的安全秘密地点。这一切始于——不出所料——冷战期间。托马斯·里德曾是里根总统的国家安全政策助理。他负责构思如果核武器击中华盛顿该怎么办。他是提出“指定幸存者”想法的人。

00:49:48.680 --> 00:50:01.720
<v Thomas Reed>Washington was targeted with over 300 Soviet nuclear weapons. They had hundreds laid that were going to lay down on every possible escape route. And to think you could escape that barrage was nonsense.
<v Thomas Reed>华盛顿被超过300枚苏联核武器瞄准。他们部署了数百枚导弹，准备覆盖每一个可能的撤离路线。所以认为你可以逃脱那阵弹幕简直是胡说八道。

00:50:01.720 --> 00:50:11.000
<v Bill Richardson>Well, I was nervous in that I remember saying, "Jeez, I know this is not going to happen. What if something does happen?"
<v Bill Richardson>嗯，我很紧张，因为我记得我说过：“天哪，我知道这不会发生。但如果真的发生了怎么办？”

00:50:11.000 --> 00:50:30.340
<v Stephanie Foo>That's Bill Richardson, former Secretary of Energy, designated survivor for the 2000 State of the Union. Richardson wasn't sent into a bunker somewhere. He was in a small town in Maryland having dinner at his friend's house, which was surrounded by a security detail and emergency vehicles. Inside, they had steak.
<v Stephanie Foo>那是比尔·理查森，前能源部长，2000年国情咨文的指定幸存者。理查森并没有被送进什么地堡。他在马里兰州的一个小镇上朋友家里吃晚饭，那里被安保分队和紧急车辆包围。在屋里，他们吃的是牛排。

00:50:30.340 --> 00:50:41.010
<v Bill Richardson>We had a nice meal. But then we sat in front of the television, and I was smoking a cigar. I had a glass of wine after it was all over.
<v Bill Richardson>我们吃了一顿美餐。但后来我们坐在电视机前，我抽着雪茄。一切结束后我喝了一杯酒。

00:50:41.010 --> 00:51:04.840
<v Stephanie Foo>I talked to a couple of designated survivors from the Cold War era, who said they were put on planes and flown to undisclosed locations. One of them got training where he got to play war games in this secret Doctor Strangelove -esque setting. And they asked him questions like, "OK, do you swear in immediately after the President's death? Do you launch missiles at the Soviets? Do you hold your fire?"
<v Stephanie Foo>我和冷战时代的几位指定幸存者聊过，他们说他们被送上飞机飞往未公开的地点。其中一人接受了训练，在那设置如同《奇爱博士》般的秘密基地里玩战争游戏。他们问他诸如：“好了，总统死后你是否立即宣誓就职？你是否对苏联发射导弹？还是按兵不动？”

00:51:04.840 --> 00:51:20.130
<v Stephanie Foo>He said the training was so helpful, so enlightening, that he thought nobody should become a designated survivor without undergoing it first. But by the end of the Cold War-- post-Gorbachev, pre-9/11-- things got a little more lax.
<v Stephanie Foo>他说训练非常有帮助，非常深刻，他认为没有人应该在不接受训练的情况下成为指定幸存者。但在冷战结束时——戈尔巴乔夫之后，9/11之前——情况变得松懈了一些。

00:51:20.130 --> 00:51:25.040
<v Dan Glickman>Can you say that I went through any kind of training course about what to do? The answer is no.
<v Dan Glickman>你能说我接受过任何关于该怎么做的培训课程吗？答案是不能。

00:51:25.040 --> 00:51:31.500
<v Stephanie Foo>That's Dan Glickman, Secretary of Agriculture, designated survivor during the 1997 State of the Union.
<v Stephanie Foo>那是丹·格利克曼，农业部长，1997年国情咨文的指定幸存者。

00:51:31.500 --> 00:51:38.780
<v Dan Glickman>I think I did discuss it with my wife, that I could handle it something terrible happened. And she says, "Well, good luck."
<v Dan Glickman>我想我和妻子讨论过，如果发生了可怕的事情，我能应付。她说：“好吧，祝你好运。”

00:51:38.780 --> 00:51:52.170
<v Stephanie Foo>Donna Shalala, who was Secretary of Health and Human Services, designated survivor in 1996, didn't get any special training either. And she didn't even leave the neighborhood. She was just two and a half miles away from the Capitol-- at the White House.
<v Stephanie Foo>唐娜·沙拉拉，曾任卫生与公众服务部长，1996年的指定幸存者，也没有接受过任何特殊训练。她甚至没离开附近。她离国会大厦只有2.5英里——在白宫。

00:51:52.170 --> 00:52:02.830
<v Donna Shalala>So I took my senior staff to the Roosevelt Room at the White House, and we had pizza and watched the State of the Union. And I sort of wandered into the Oval Office and tried out the chair.
<v Donna Shalala>所以我带了高级职员去白宫的罗斯福厅，我们吃了披萨，观看了国情咨文。我有点溜进了椭圆形办公室，试坐了那把椅子。

00:52:02.830 --> 00:52:04.450
<v Stephanie Foo>What did it feel like?
<v Stephanie Foo>感觉怎么样？

00:52:04.450 --> 00:52:13.800
<v Donna Shalala>Big. Too big for me. President Clinton's a very large man.
<v Donna Shalala>很大。对我来说太大了。克林顿总统是个非常高大的人。

00:52:13.800 --> 00:52:28.740
<v Stephanie Foo>One designated survivor told me that actually attending the State of the Union is a pain. You have to look attentive and interested and approving for a really long time, in case the cameras pan to you. Being designated survivor? Much easier.
<v Stephanie Foo>一位指定幸存者告诉我，实际上出席国情咨文是一种折磨。你必须长时间保持专注、感兴趣且赞同，以防摄像机扫到你。成为指定幸存者？容易多了。

00:52:28.740 --> 00:52:48.900
<v Stephanie Foo>One guy, who asked I not use his name, went on a vacation to the Caribbean when he got the gig. Not a government expense, he was quick to tell me. He happened to have plans to go anyway. Another designated survivor took the opportunity to move to a new house. A caravan of Secret Service guarded him as he loaded up his moving van.
<v Stephanie Foo>一个人，要求我不使用他的名字，在他接到这个任务时去加勒比海度假。他很快告诉我这不是政府买单，他本来就计划去。另一位指定幸存者借机搬到了新家。特勤局的车队守护着他，帮他装好搬家货车。

00:52:48.900 --> 00:53:12.740
<v Stephanie Foo>One thing you might have noticed from the designated survivors I spoke to-- none of them had day jobs that had anything to do with national security. Usually it's the non-marquee Cabinet members-- Secretary of the Interior, Energy, Commerce, Agriculture-- people whose names you probably don't know. Ever heard of Anthony Foxx? He's our Secretary of Transportation-- and this year's designated survivor.
<v Stephanie Foo>你可能从我采访过的指定幸存者那里注意到一件事——他们中没有人的日常工作与国家安全有关。通常是那些非头牌的内阁成员——内政部长、能源、商务、农业部长——你可能不知道名字的人。听说过安东尼·福克斯吗？他是我们的交通部长——也是今年的指定幸存者。

00:53:12.740 --> 00:53:32.100
<v Stephanie Foo>And when I heard about that, I kind of thought, well, seriously? The decision to launch nuclear retaliation, kill millions of people, could possibly rest in the hands of the Secretary of Transportation? Thomas Reed, the guy who designed this program, says, "Why not? The Secretary of Transportation is as good a person as any."
<v Stephanie Foo>当我听到这个时，我心想，好吧，认真的吗？发动核报复、杀害数百万人这样决定的权利，竟然可能掌握在交通部长手中？托马斯·里德，设计这个项目的人说：“为什么不呢？交通部长和任何人一样好。”

00:53:32.100 --> 00:53:51.540
<v Thomas Reed>The question in politics, and all other things in life, is, "Compared to who?" The question is not, "Is So-and-so authorized or qualified to be President, or Governor, or successor? The question is, "Compared to who?" Compared to Joseph Stalin? Of course. Compared to Dwight Eisenhower? Probably not.
<v Thomas Reed>政治以及生活中所有事情的问题在于：“和谁比？”问题不在于：“某某是否有权或有资格担任总统、州长或继任者？”问题是：“和谁比？”和斯大林比？当然有资格。和艾森豪威尔比？可能没资格。

00:53:51.540 --> 00:54:00.270
<v Stephanie Foo>And the Cabinet, they're all brilliant people, and certainly have the wherewithal to at least make the initial decisions.
<v Stephanie Foo>内阁成员都是聪明人，而且肯定有能力至少做出初步决定。

00:54:00.270 --> 00:54:15.890
<v Thomas Reed>[CHUCKLES QUIETLY] Stephanie, they're not all brilliant people, any more than you or I are. And they all have problems, and some smoke too much, and some are overweight, and so forth and so on. They're all human beings.
<v Thomas Reed>[轻轻笑] 斯蒂芬妮，他们不是所有都是聪明人，就像你我一样。他们都有问题，有些人烟抽太多，有些人超重等等。他们都是凡人。

00:54:15.890 --> 00:54:26.210
<v Stephanie Foo>And, Reed said, the President's been chosen by the American people, and the President chooses the designated survivor. So therefore, by proxy, that person is chosen by the American people.
<v Stephanie Foo>里德说，总统是由美国人民选出的，而总统选择指定幸存者。所以，通过这种代理，那个人也是由美国人民选出的。

00:54:26.210 --> 00:54:30.560
<v Thomas Reed>Sometimes they choose well. Sometimes they don't. That's what politics is all about.
<v Thomas Reed>有时他们选得好，有时选不好。这就是政治的一切。

00:54:30.560 --> 00:54:32.280
<v Stephanie Foo>That's a very ominous statement.
<v Stephanie Foo>那是一个非常不祥的陈述。

00:54:32.280 --> 00:54:36.560
<v Thomas Reed>[LAUGHS] Yes. Yes, it is.
<v Thomas Reed>[笑] 是的，是的。

00:54:36.560 --> 00:54:40.500
<v Stephanie Foo>Here's Dan Glickman again, designated survivor, 1997, Secretary of Agriculture.
<v Stephanie Foo>这是丹·格利克曼，1997年指定幸存者，农业部长。

00:54:40.500 --> 00:54:56.650
<v Dan Glickman>You know, I mean, I was not really briefed on what to do if something were to happen to the President. But you know, I have enough confidence in myself that if something were to happen, I would have followed certain basic rules, and would have been a good soldier, in that case.
<v Dan Glickman>你知道，我的意思是，如果总统出了什么事，我并没有真正收到关于该做什么的简报。但你知道，我有足够的自信，如果发生了什么，我会遵循某些基本原则，在那情况下，我会成为一名好士兵。

00:54:56.650 --> 00:55:21.590
<v Stephanie Foo>When Glickman was a designated survivor, they flew him to LaGuardia Airport in a G3 plane, along with some stern-looking guys in suits-- one of whom may or may not have been carrying the nuclear football in a fancy briefcase. Then they got Glickman in a three-car caravan, drove him to his daughter's modest apartment in Manhattan's West Village, and stood guard outside while he watched the speech on TV. Glickman admitted he did imagine what it would be like.
<v Stephanie Foo>当格利克曼担任指定幸存者时，他们用G3飞机把他送到了拉瓜迪亚机场，随行的还有几个穿着西装看起来很严肃的家伙——其中一个人可能正提着装有核密码箱的精美公文包。然后他们安排格利克曼坐上三辆车的车队，开车把他送到曼哈顿西村他女儿那间简朴的公寓，并在外面守卫，而他在电视上观看了演讲。格利克曼承认他确实想象过那会是什么样子。

00:55:21.590 --> 00:55:36.760
<v Dan Glickman>I did think about it. It was a very interesting thing that here, the Secretary of Agriculture could be President of the United States. And I'd been telling the world all these years that agriculture was the most important part of our government, so you know, I could reinforce that message, certainly.
<v Dan Glickman>我确实想过。这很有趣，这里，农业部长竟然可能成为美国总统。这些年来我一直告诉世界农业是我们政府最重要的部分，所以你知道，我当然可以加强那个信息。

00:55:36.760 --> 00:55:42.530
<v Stephanie Foo>[LAUGHS] All of a sudden, that would be our number-one national agenda, is just agriculture.
<v Stephanie Foo>[笑] 突然间，那将成为我们国家的第一号日程，就是农业。

00:55:42.530 --> 00:55:44.730
<v Dan Glickman>Agriculture. Food and agriculture.
<v Dan Glickman>农业。食品和农业。

00:55:44.730 --> 00:55:54.320
<v Stephanie Foo>Glickman even said that in his regular job, he was so unimportant that immediately after the speech was over, he lost the security detail. They bailed on him and went back to Washington.
<v Stephanie Foo>格利克曼甚至说，在他常规工作中，他是如此不重要，以至于演讲结束后，他立刻就失去了安保分队。他们抛下他回了华盛顿。

00:55:54.320 --> 00:56:11.680
<v Dan Glickman>In fact, their exact words were, "Mr. Glickman, the mission is terminated." And I decided to stay in New York and have dinner with my daughter. And it was cold and raining and sleeting, and once the dinner was over, we went outside of the restaurant, and we couldn't find a cab.
<v Dan Glickman>事实上，他们的原话是：“格利克曼先生，任务已终止。”我决定留在纽约和我女儿吃晚饭。外面又冷又下着雨夹雪，晚饭结束后，我们走出餐厅，找不到出租车。

00:56:11.680 --> 00:56:30.140
<v Dan Glickman>So we walked back, about 12 blocks, back to my daughter's apartment in a sleet storm. And it struck me that just three or four hours before, I was the most powerful man on the face of the Earth, for about an hour. And now I couldn't even get a cab.
<v Dan Glickman>所以我们在雨夹雪中步行了大约12个街区回到女儿的公寓。我突然意识到，就在三四个小时前，我还是地球上最有权势的人，大约一小时。现在我连车都打不到。

00:56:30.140 --> 00:56:38.170
<v Stephanie Foo>The fact of the matter is, no first-term president goes into office knowing how to be president. He learns on the job. Everything's new.
<v Stephanie Foo>事实是，没有一个第一任总统在上任时就知道如何当总统。他是边干边学的。一切都是新的。

00:56:38.170 --> 00:57:00.950
<v Stephanie Foo>And opening that briefcase and deciding whether to launch a nuclear strike? Nobody could be ready for that. The President might be as likely to make the wrong choice as the Secretary of Agriculture. Or the Secretary of Transportation, or the White House intern, or you, or I.
<v Stephanie Foo>至于打开那个公文包决定是否发动核打击？没有人能对此做好准备。总统犯错的可能性可能和农业部长一样大。或者交通部长、白宫实习生，或者你，或者我。

00:57:00.950 --> 00:57:04.890
<v Ira Glass>Stephanie Foo is one of the producers of our program.
<v Ira Glass>斯蒂芬妮·傅是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:57:04.890 --> 00:57:06.810
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "I DON'T KNOW WHAT I'M DOING" BY DEAN MARTIN]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 迪恩·马丁的《我不知道我在做什么》]

00:57:06.810 --> 00:57:16.840
<v Ira Glass>ACT 4:
<v Ira Glass>第四幕：

00:57:16.840 --> 00:57:31.560
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Stephanie Foo with Zoe Chace, Sean Cole, Chana Joffe-Walt, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, Brian Reed, Joe Richman, Robyn Semien, Alissa Shipp, and Nancy Updike. Our senior producer for today's show  is Julie Snyder. Editing help from Joel Lovell. Technical director is Matt Tierney. Production help from Lyra Smith.
<v Ira Glass>好的，我们的节目今天由斯蒂芬妮·傅制作，佐伊·蔡斯、肖恩·科尔、查娜·乔夫-沃尔特、米基·米克、乔纳森·门吉瓦尔、布莱恩·里德、乔·里奇曼、罗宾·塞门、阿丽莎·希普和南希·厄普代克参与。今天节目的高级制作人是朱莉·斯奈德。乔尔·洛弗尔提供编辑帮助。技术总监是马特·蒂尔尼。莱拉·史密斯提供制作帮助。

00:57:31.560 --> 00:57:36.650
<v Ira Glass>Research help today from Christopher Swetala and Michelle Harris. Music help from Damien Graef and Rob Geddis.
<v Ira Glass>今天克里斯托弗·斯威塔拉和米歇尔·哈里斯提供研究帮助。达米安·格拉夫和罗布·格迪斯提供音乐帮助。

00:57:36.650 --> 00:57:47.240
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks today to Derrick Brown for telling us about the Soldier Safety Show at Fort Bragg. Derrick goes on tour with Greg Dulli and The Afghan Whigs this March. Find out where at brownpoetry.com.
<v Ira Glass>特别感谢德里克·布朗向我们讲述布拉格堡的士兵安全秀。德里克将于今年3月与格雷格·杜利和阿富汗辉格乐队巡演。在brownpoetry.com上可以查到巡演地点。

00:57:47.240 --> 00:58:01.380
<v Ira Glass>Thanks to Lisa Pollak for cluing us in about designated survivors. Thanks to Joanna Solotaroff, Alex Kapelman, Maria DiCondina, Brian Haley, Rusty Hodgkinson, Quincy Billups, Victor Hurtado, Evan Middlesworth at Pine Hollow Audio, and the teachers and students at Glen Ridge High School.
<v Ira Glass>感谢丽莎·波拉克为我们提供有关指定幸存者的线索。感谢乔安娜·索洛塔罗夫、亚历克斯·卡佩尔曼、玛丽亚·迪康迪纳、布莱恩·黑利、拉斯蒂·霍金森、昆西·比拉普斯、维克多·乌尔塔多、埃文·米德尔斯沃思在Pine Hollow Audio的工作，以及格伦里奇高中的师生们。

00:58:01.380 --> 00:58:08.650
<v Ira Glass>Our website, thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站，thisamericanlife.org。《美国生活》由PRX（公共广播交易所）分发给公共广播电台。

00:58:08.650 --> 00:58:16.710
<v Ira Glass>Thanks as always to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia, who hears his name right here in the credits every single week-- every single week. And he asks--
<v Ira Glass>像往常一样感谢我们节目的联合创始人托里·马拉蒂亚先生，他每周——每周都在职员表中听到自己的名字。他问——

00:58:16.710 --> 00:58:21.000
<v Derrick Brown>What the-- what are we doing? Why are we doing this?
<v Derrick Brown>到底——我们在做什么？为什么我们要这么做？

00:58:21.000 --> 00:58:25.560
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带给你更多《美国生活》的故事。

00:58:25.560 --> 00:58:30.560
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "I DON'T KNOW WHAT I'M DOING" BY DEAN MARTIN]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 迪恩·马丁的《我不知道我在做什么》]
